  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 310 AM EDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nAny lingering showers will continue to diminish through the \npredawn hours as the departing low pulls farther into eastern \nCanada and surface high pressure begins building southeast into \nthe Great Lakes. This marks the beginning of a quiet stretch of \nweather through at least the latter half of the work week as \nCanadian High pressure settles overhead beneath a persistent \nnorthwest flow aloft. While the larger scale upper-level pattern\nmay fluctuate somewhat with time, little overall change is \nexpected through much of the forecast period, favoring a \ncontinued stretch of generally dry and seasonable conditions. \n\nToday looks pleasant with sunshine building in through the day \nand lower humidity levels, although a tightening pressure \ngradient between the aformentioned exiting low and incoming high\npressure will support breezy north-northwest winds through much\nof the day. Gusts should be sufficient enough to build waves \nalong portions of the Lake Michigan and Lake Huron shoreline, \nwhere hazardous swimming conditions may develop. Winds look to \nease Tuesday night as high pressure works overhead, allowing for\nefficient radiational cooling. The coolest morning s appear to \nbe Wednesday and Thursday, when several of the traditional \ncolder interior locations could dip into the 40s before quickly \nrebounding after sunrise. Despite the cool starts, afternoon \ntemperatures should steadily moderate with many locations \nclimbing back into the 70s and 80s by Thursday under plentiful \nsunshine. \n\nAttention then turns toward late week and the weekend as \nnorthwest flow persists and a couple of subtle disturbances \nattempt to work southeast across the Upper Great Lakes. \nConfidence remains lower regarding the timing and coverage of \nany precipitation, though the current signal continues to favor \nat least low chances for showers and perhaps a few thunderstorms\nsometime Friday into Saturday. Outside of any passing \nprecipitation chances, temperatures should remain close to \nseasonal normals through the weekend and into early next week \nwith no strong indication of prolonged heat or widespread \nimpactful weather at this time.",
